<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taejo of Goguryeo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taejodae of Goguryeo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taejo was the sixth king of Goguryeo, the ancient Korean kingdom, whose traditional reign of 53 to 146 CE is implausibly long. Under him Goguryeo became a more centralised kingdom, subjugated the Okjeo and Dongye peoples on the east coast and raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> and Xuantu. He is often regarded as the first king under whom Goguryeo emerged as a true state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under King <b>Taejo</b>, whose traditional reign of 53–146 is implausibly long, Goguryeo subjugated the Okjeo and Dongye peoples to the east and began to raid the Chinese commanderies of Liaodong and Xuantu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As King <b>Taejo</b> (r. 918–943) he pursued a patient policy of alliances, marrying into some twenty-nine powerful families and winning over local lords with titles, land and the royal surname.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1392 he deposed the last Goryeo king, Gongyang, and took the throne; he is known as <b>Taejo</b> (r. 1392–1398).</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Goguryeo made Okjeo a tributary under King <b>Taejo</b>, traditionally in 56 CE, and later absorbed it fully, drawing on its marine products.</blockquote><a href="/state/okjeo">Okjeo</a></li></ul></section>
